\title{Isomorphisms of Meaning:\\Structural Invariance, Analogical Transport, and Semantic Underdetermination}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We separate two claims often conflated in discussions of structuralism and analogy: invariance of truth under structural equivalence and invariance of meaning. We study relational modal models consisting of worlds, a transition relation, and a valuation of atomic observations. An isomorphism simultaneously renames worlds and atoms while preserving transitions and valuations. Satisfaction of every formula generated from atoms, falsity, implication, and necessity is invariant under such an isomorphism. Consequently, global validity is invariant and truth transport is stable under composition. Identity, reversal, and composition organize model isomorphisms into a groupoid. Conjugation by changes of coordinates gives a precise ``isomorphism of isomorphisms'' and preserves transported truth.

We then add an interpretation layer external to the model signature. Two one-world, one-atom models are structurally identical and agree on every modal formula, yet receive opposite Boolean interpretations. Structural equivalence therefore preserves every truth expressible in the fixed observational language but need not preserve unconstrained extra-structural meaning. This conclusion is intentionally language-relative: extending the vocabulary to name the interpretation removes the indistinguishability. We discuss consequences for analogical reasoning, representation independence, state-transition systems, scientific models, and Copycat-style architectures, and present finite algorithms for evaluation and transport.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Structural reasoning identifies systems by patterns of relations rather than names. A graph remains the same after its vertices are permuted; a transition system retains its behavior after states are consistently renamed; and one mathematical model may recur in several sciences under different interpretations. Isomorphism captures this invariance by specifying a reversible correspondence preserving the selected structure.

A stronger inference sometimes follows: structurally indistinguishable systems must have the same meaning. That conclusion is not licensed without specifying what is observable and where interpretation resides. Modal semantics makes the distinction exact. We allow isomorphisms to rename both states and atomic vocabulary. The positive result says that every statement in the fixed language survives this transport. The negative result says that an arbitrary interpretation omitted from the language need not survive.

The algebra of correspondence adds a second theme. Isomorphisms have identities, inverses, and composites and thus form a groupoid. An analogy can itself be re-expressed by changing coordinates at its source and target. This provides a rigorous core for analogical architectures: reversible correspondence, compositional transport, and invariance under representation change.

\section{Modal relational models}

\begin{definition}[Formula]
Let $A$ be a set of atomic observations. The formulas $\mathcal F(A)$ are generated by
\[
 \varphi ::= a\mid\bot\mid(\varphi\to\psi)\mid\Box\varphi,
 \qquad a\in A.
\]
Negation and possibility may be defined by $\neg\varphi:=\varphi\to\bot$ and $\Diamond\varphi:=\neg\Box\neg\varphi$.
\end{definition}

A bijection $g:A\to B$ induces a renaming $g_*:\mathcal F(A)\to\mathcal F(B)$ recursively:
\[
\begin{aligned}
g_*(a)&=g(a), & g_*(\bot)&=\bot,\\
g_*(\varphi\to\psi)&=g_*(\varphi)\to g_*(\psi),&
g_*(\Box\varphi)&=\Box g_*(\varphi).
\end{aligned}
\]
The logical shape and modal depth are unchanged.

\begin{definition}[Relational modal model]
A model over $A$ is a triple $M=(W,R,V)$, where $W$ is a set of worlds, $R\subseteq W\times W$ is an accessibility relation, and $V:W\times A\to\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$ is a valuation.
\end{definition}

Satisfaction is recursively defined by
\[
\begin{aligned}
M,w\models a&\iff V(w,a),\\
M,w\models\bot&\iff \mathrm{false},\\
M,w\models\varphi\to\psi&\iff (M,w\models\varphi)\Rightarrow(M,w\models\psi),\\
M,w\models\Box\varphi&\iff \forall x\in W,\ R(w,x)\Rightarrow M,x\models\varphi.
\end{aligned}
\]
A formula is valid in $M$ if it is satisfied at every world.

\begin{definition}[Model isomorphism]
Let $M=(W,R,V)$ be over $A$ and $N=(X,S,U)$ be over $B$. A model isomorphism $e:M\cong N$ consists of bijections $f:W\to X$ and $g:A\to B$ such that, for all $w,x\in W$ and $a\in A$,
\[
S(f(w),f(x))\iff R(w,x),\qquad
U(f(w),g(a))\iff V(w,a).
\]
\end{definition}

Both directions matter. A forward embedding may leave additional target successors uncontrolled, whereas a modal box quantifies over every successor.

\section{Structural invariance}

\begin{theorem}[Pointed structural invariance]
Let $e:M\cong N$ have world bijection $f$ and atom bijection $g$. For every $w\in W$ and $\varphi\in\mathcal F(A)$,
\[
N,f(w)\models g_*(\varphi)\iff M,w\models\varphi.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use structural induction on $\varphi$. The atomic case is valuation preservation, and the falsity case is immediate. For implication, apply the induction hypotheses to antecedent and consequent.

For $\varphi=\Box\alpha$, assume first $M,w\models\Box\alpha$ and take $y\in X$ with $S(f(w),y)$. Surjectivity gives $y=f(x)$ for $x=f^{-1}(y)$. Reflection of transitions gives $R(w,x)$, hence $M,x\models\alpha$. The induction hypothesis yields $N,y\models g_*(\alpha)$. Thus $N,f(w)\models\Box g_*(\alpha)$.

Conversely, assume $N,f(w)\models\Box g_*(\alpha)$ and take $x$ with $R(w,x)$. Transition preservation gives $S(f(w),f(x))$; the target box gives $N,f(x)\models g_*(\alpha)$; and induction gives $M,x\models\alpha$. Therefore $M,w\models\Box\alpha$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Invariance of valid theories]
Under the same hypotheses, for every $\varphi\in\mathcal F(A)$,
\[
(\forall y\in X,\ N,y\models g_*(\varphi))
\iff
(\forall w\in W,\ M,w\models\varphi).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
In one direction evaluate target validity at $f(w)$ and apply the theorem. In the other, write arbitrary $y$ as $f(f^{-1}(y))$, use source validity at $f^{-1}(y)$, and apply the theorem in reverse.
\end{proof}

Thus isomorphic models share their entire transported modal theory, not merely a selected finite collection of observations.

\section{The groupoid of analogies}
Every model $M$ has an identity isomorphism $1_M$. Every $e:M\cong N$ has an inverse $e^{-1}:N\cong M$. If $e:M\cong N$ has maps $(f,g)$ and $h:N\cong P$ has maps $(k,\ell)$, their composite has maps $(k\circ f,\ell\circ g)$. Preservation follows by chaining biconditionals. Associativity, identity, and inverse laws follow from those for bijections, so models and isomorphisms form a groupoid.

\begin{theorem}[Compositional truth transport]
Let $e:M\cong N$ and $h:N\cong P$. For every world $w$ of $M$ and formula $\varphi$ over its atoms,
\[
P,(h\circ e)(w)\models(h\circ e)_*(\varphi)
\iff M,w\models\varphi.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The composite bijections preserve transitions and valuations. Apply pointed structural invariance to the composite. Equivalently, apply it successively to $e$ and $h$, observing $(h\circ e)_*=h_*\circ e_*$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Isomorphisms of isomorphisms}
Let
\[
L:M\cong M',\qquad E:M\cong N,\qquad R:N\cong N'.
\]
The coordinate-transported analogy is
\[
E^{L,R}=R\circ E\circ L^{-1}:M'\cong N'.
\]
It is an ``isomorphism of isomorphisms'' in the groupoid sense: $L$ and $R$ re-present the endpoints while conjugation re-expresses the arrow.

\begin{theorem}[Conjugated-analogy invariance]
For every world $w'$ of $M'$ and every formula $\psi$ over its atoms,
\[
N',E^{L,R}(w')\models E^{L,R}_*(\psi)
\iff M',w'\models\psi.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The inverse $L^{-1}$, the analogy $E$, and the endpoint change $R$ are isomorphisms. Their composite is an isomorphism $M'\cong N'$. Apply compositional truth transport.
\end{proof}

The theorem captures representation independence for correspondences themselves. Re-encoding both domains does not invalidate an analogy if the correspondence is transported coherently.

\section{External interpretation and semantic collision}

\begin{definition}[Interpreted model]
Let $D$ be a set of meanings. An interpreted model is a pair $\mathcal I=(M,I)$, where $M=(W,R,V)$ is a relational model and $I:W\to D$ is an external interpretation. The map $I$ is deliberately absent from the formula grammar, accessibility relation, and valuation.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Meaning compatibility]
For $\mathcal I=(M,I)$ and $\mathcal J=(N,J)$, an isomorphism $e:M\cong N$ with world map $f$ is meaning-compatible if
\[
J(f(w))=I(w)
\]
for every world $w$ of $M$.
\end{definition}

Consider $W=\{*\}$ and $A=\{p\}$. Let the sole transition and sole atomic valuation be true:
\[
R(*,*)=\mathrm{true},\qquad V(*,p)=\mathrm{true}.
\]
Call the resulting model $S$. Define interpreted copies
\[
\mathcal I_0=(S,I_0),\quad I_0(*)=\mathrm{false},
\qquad
\mathcal I_1=(S,I_1),\quad I_1(*)=\mathrm{true}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Meaning collision]
There are two interpreted models and a structural isomorphism between their underlying models such that every modal formula agrees at corresponding worlds, the external interpretations are unequal, and the isomorphism is not meaning-compatible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use $\mathcal I_0$ and $\mathcal I_1$. Identity maps on the singleton world and atom sets form a structural isomorphism. Pointed structural invariance gives agreement for every $\varphi\in\mathcal F(A)$. Nevertheless,
\[
I_1(*)=\mathrm{true}\ne\mathrm{false}=I_0(*),
\]
so meaning compatibility fails.
\end{proof}

The example is nonvacuous: the world exists, the atom is observed, the transition relation is populated, and the comparison ranges over formulas of arbitrary size and depth. Yet the conclusion is language-relative. If the vocabulary is enriched by an atom $q$ satisfying
\[
V_i(*,q)\iff I_i(*)=\mathrm{true},
\]
then $q$ distinguishes the models. The precise boundary is that an arbitrary annotation omitted from an observational signature is not determined by all truths expressible in that signature.

\section{Finite algorithms}
For finite systems, the constructions are computationally direct.

\paragraph{Formula evaluation.}
Evaluate syntax recursively. With memoization keyed by world--subformula pairs, each pair is processed once. If $n=|W|$, $m=|R|$, and $s$ is the number of distinct formula nodes, the time is $O(s(n+m))$ and storage is $O(sn)$.

\paragraph{Checking a proposed isomorphism.}
Verify that the world and atom maps are bijections. Then test every transition and valuation equation. Dense tables require $O(n^2+nk)$ time for $n$ worlds and $k$ atoms, with $O(n+k)$ auxiliary storage. Finding maps from scratch may require graph-isomorphism-style search; naive enumeration has up to $n!k!$ candidates, while partition refinement can reduce the search.

\paragraph{Transport and conjugation.}
Formula transport visits every syntax node and takes $O(s)$ time. Composition and inversion of explicit permutation tables are linear in domain size. Conjugating an analogy computes $R\circ E\circ L^{-1}$ separately on worlds and atoms and is linear after inverse tables are available.

These procedures manipulate only the stated signature. They cannot discover an interpretation excluded from their input.

\section{Applications and discussion}
In representation-independent software, state identifiers and proposition names may change while safety and inevitability properties remain fixed. Structural invariance supplies the guarantee; conjugated-analogy invariance preserves mappings after independent re-encoding.

Scientific analogies reuse relational forms across circuits, fluids, populations, and transportation. Such reuse transports deductions in the shared vocabulary, but does not identify voltage with pressure or population with charge. Domain interpretation remains additional information unless represented structurally.

Database schemas offer another example. Permuted records and fields can preserve all represented relations while business meanings differ. Integration therefore needs meaning-compatible mappings or metadata rich enough to expose the distinction.

For Copycat-style analogy, the groupoid calculus supplies an exact baseline. Correspondences can be reversed, chained, and reframed while invariant observations remain stable. It does not yet model salience, competition, probabilistic confidence, or contextual slippage. Those mechanisms concern selection among correspondences; the present theorems concern conclusions guaranteed once an exact correspondence is fixed.

Isomorphism is always relative to a signature. Objects may be isomorphic with respect to adjacency and nonisomorphic after colors, weights, time, or interpretations are added. The positive and negative theorems are therefore complementary. Structural invariance covers every internal observation; semantic collision varies an external coordinate.

\section{Future research}
Finite characteristic formulas may determine when an observational language classifies pointed models up to isomorphism and how modal depth measures identification cost. Graded modalities are natural candidates.

Networks of analogies raise a coherence question. Transport around cycles may produce holonomy; globally consistent meaning assignments should correspond to trivial holonomy, perhaps testable on a cycle basis.

Approximate analogies in probabilistic transition systems should replace equality by error bounds. Modal depth is expected to control error accumulation because nested boxes repeatedly expose transition defects.

The collision theorem also suggests an orbit criterion. Any interpretation recoverable from invariant truth must be constant on automorphism orbits. For finite models, counting modalities may make orbit constancy sufficient for definability.

Finally, bisimulation should be compared with isomorphism as a measure of semantic resolution. Modal logic often sees exactly bisimulation-invariant distinctions while ignoring identity and cardinal information. Matching languages to their natural equivalences would extend the present boundary analysis.

\section{Conclusion}
A reversible renaming of worlds and atoms preserving transitions and valuations preserves every modal truth. The result extends to global validity, chains of analogies, and conjugated correspondences. Exact analogies therefore possess coherent groupoid structure.

External interpretation can nevertheless vary while all those observations remain fixed. The singleton collision isolates the boundary: truth preservation is indexed by an observational language. Within that language, isomorphism is decisive; beyond it, semantic claims require additional structure, vocabulary, or grounding.

\end{document}

