# Isomorphisms of Meaning: an interactive journey

Begin with the central puzzle: when does a change of names preserve everything that can be said? Explore the [Stanford Encyclopedia overview of modal logic](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-modal/) for background, then operate the coordinate switchboard.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Inspect the same structure

{{visualization:0}}

The first computational experiment checks a nontrivial finite model point by point and formula by formula.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the definition of structural isomorphism</summary>A structural isomorphism is a pair of bijections on worlds and atoms that preserves and reflects every transition and every atomic valuation. Both directions matter because necessity quantifies over all target successors.</details>

## How truth is evaluated

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the induction behind truth invariance</summary>Atoms follow from valuation preservation; falsity is immediate; implication follows from the two induction hypotheses. For a box formula, the inverse world bijection turns an arbitrary target successor into a source successor, where the induction hypothesis applies.</details>

## Correspondences can themselves move

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

Read more about [groupoids](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groupoid), whose reversible arrows capture identity, inversion, composition, and coordinate transport.

<details><summary>Reveal the conjugated-analogy theorem</summary>If L changes source coordinates, E is the original analogy, and R changes target coordinates, then R ∘ E ∘ L⁻¹ is again a structural isomorphism. The structural invariance theorem therefore applies to every transported formula.</details>

## Where meaning escapes

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

Two identical one-world models can receive opposite external labels. No increase in formula depth discovers a bit that the observational vocabulary never represented.

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact limitation</summary>The claim is relative to the fixed modal language, not to every conceivable language. Add an atom naming the external label and the models become distinguishable. The collision identifies a definability boundary rather than an absolute impossibility of semantic description.</details>

## Takeaway

Structural correspondence preserves all truths stated in the shared observational language. Choosing and grounding that language remains a separate semantic task.

